WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.599
If you're building MCP servers that run in production, log notifications, progress notifications,

2
00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:12.399
and routes are the three features that matter most. They let servers communicate status during

3
00:00:12.399 --> 00:00:18.239
long-running operations and give servers controlled, scoped access to the client file system

4
00:00:18.239 --> 00:00:25.360
without opening up a security loophole. Let's go through each one now. A slow tool call and a hung

5
00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.280
tool call look exactly the same from the client side, and that ambiguity is what these features

6
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.240
fix. A progress notification is an MCP notification a server sends during a long-running tool call

7
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.959
to report incremental status. It carries a numeric progress value and an optional total,

8
00:00:44.959 --> 00:00:50.959
so the client can compute a percentage for the user. A log notification carries diagnostic text

9
00:00:50.959 --> 00:00:58.720
with a severity level, such as info, warning, or error, plus an optional logger name. And routes

10
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:04.239
are the list of file system paths, or URIs the client advises a server to work within.

11
00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.480
Routes communicate intended scope, but they are advisory because the protocol doesn't enforce them.

12
00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:17.760
The standard MCP tool call model is synchronous. The client sends a request and waits for the result.

13
00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:23.199
For tools that finish in milliseconds, that's completely fine, but for tools that process large

14
00:01:23.199 --> 00:01:30.000
files, run database queries, or make external API calls, the client can be waiting for seconds or

15
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.400
even minutes with no feedback. That silence creates a bad user experience and makes it hard to diagnose

16
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:43.199
problems when something goes wrong. Notifications break that silence. The server sends status updates

17
00:01:43.199 --> 00:01:48.959
as the operation progresses, and the client can display them, log them, or surface them to the user

18
00:01:48.959 --> 00:01:56.000
in real time. Think of notifications as the MCP equivalent of a progress bar. They transform an

19
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:02.239
opaque weight into an observable process. Log notifications are the simpler of the two

20
00:02:02.239 --> 00:02:06.959
notification types. They carry a severity level, a logger name, and a message string.

21
00:02:07.519 --> 00:02:14.479
These are the eight standard size log severity levels. Debug, info, notice, warning, error,

22
00:02:14.479 --> 00:02:20.800
critical, alert, and emergency, in increasing order of urgency. The server sends these whenever it

23
00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:26.559
wants to surface diagnostic information to the client. The client can display them in a log pane,

24
00:02:26.559 --> 00:02:32.800
filter by severity threshold, store them for later review, or ignore them entirely. Log notifications

25
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.639
are fire and forget. They don't expect a response, so a server can omit them during any operation

26
00:02:38.639 --> 00:02:44.800
without blocking. You call a single context method and the MCP framework handles the rest,

27
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.080
especially valuable during development when you want step-by-step visibility into what a tool is

28
00:02:50.080 --> 00:02:56.720
doing. Progress notifications have a slightly more structured format because they're designed to drive

29
00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.279
UI elements like progress bars. Every progress notification includes a progress token, an opaque

30
00:03:03.279 --> 00:03:09.520
identifier from the original tool call request. That token ties the update to its parent operation,

31
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:15.039
so the client knows which tool call is reporting. The progress field is a numeric value indicating

32
00:03:15.039 --> 00:03:20.960
how far along the operation is. The total field is optional. Include it, and clients can divide

33
00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.320
progress by total to get a percentage. Leave it out, and the client shows an indeterminate

34
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.759
indicator. The convention is to call report progress at meaningful checkpoints after each

35
00:03:31.759 --> 00:03:37.759
major step completes, not on every minor iteration. That way the client always has a current view

36
00:03:37.759 --> 00:03:44.559
without being flooded with updates. Wiring progress notifications into a tool is straightforward.

37
00:03:44.559 --> 00:03:49.600
When the client sends a tool call request, it can include a progress token in the meta field.

38
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.639
Inside your handler, you check for that token. If it's present, you call CTX report progress at

39
00:03:56.639 --> 00:04:02.880
each meaningful step. The MCP server framework serializes the notification and sends it to the

40
00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:09.600
client automatically. You don't manage the transport yourself. One important detail. If the client

41
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.960
didn't include a progress token, it means the client doesn't want progress updates for this call.

42
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:21.760
Skip the report progress calls. Clients that don't support progress notifications won't send

43
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:29.359
tokens, and your tool degrades gracefully with no special handling required. It's worth comparing

44
00:04:29.359 --> 00:04:34.239
log and progress notifications directly because developers sometimes reach for the wrong one.

45
00:04:34.799 --> 00:04:40.640
Log notifications are for diagnostic text, meaning messages that tell you what the server is doing

46
00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:45.600
and whether it's healthy. They're tagged with severity so they can be filtered and stored.

47
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:51.040
Progress notifications are for numeric status tied to a specific operation.

48
00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:55.839
They're designed to drive a progress indicator in the user interface. You wouldn't use a log

49
00:04:55.839 --> 00:05:00.799
notification to update a progress bar, and you wouldn't use a progress notification to surface

50
00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:07.600
an error message. In a production server, you'll typically use both log notifications for observability

51
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.559
and debugging. Progress notifications for user-facing feedback during long operations.

52
00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:19.440
Roots are how MCP communicates file system scope, and here's what people get backwards. It's the

53
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.600
client, not the server, that declares the root's capability during initialization. The server then

54
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:33.760
requests the list by calling roots slash list, and each root is a URI. Those roots are advisory.

55
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:39.839
They tell a well-behaved server where to confine itself, but the protocol never stops one from

56
00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:45.040
reaching outside. That matters most when servers come from different vendors, and you don't want

57
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:50.880
a third-party tool reading files beyond its expected scope. The protection has to be layered

58
00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:56.559
underneath through restricted operating system permissions, a sandbox around the server, or the

59
00:05:56.559 --> 00:06:03.119
client validating paths before it acts. Roots make the intended scope explicit and auditable.

60
00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:10.399
The enforcement is your job. When a long-running tool goes quiet, what the user actually sees

61
00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:16.559
is a client that looks hung up, and what you see in triage is nothing at all. That's the gap these

62
00:06:16.559 --> 00:06:24.160
features close. One carrying diagnostic out, and one carrying progress back. Roots are the odd one

63
00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:30.559
out. They tell a server where to stay, but they don't enforce it, so the real boundary still lives

64
00:06:30.559 --> 00:06:35.679
in your permissions file. That closes out this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

